description:
geocode.pb.js's onBootstrap (line ~405) is the ONLY place in the repo that touches settings.rateLimits:

  const others = settings.rateLimits.enabled ? (rules||[]).filter(notGeocode) : [];
  settings.rateLimits.enabled = true;
  settings.rateLimits.rules = others.concat(geocodeRules);

On a fresh instance PocketBase ships rateLimits.enabled = false with a set of inactive factory default rules (auth, create, per-prefix and global /api/ caps). Because enabled is false, 'others' becomes [] — those defaults are DISCARDED — and the limiter is then switched on with only the two geocode labels.

Net effect: the server runs with rate limiting nominally ENABLED while the only throttled paths are /api/federfall/geocode and /api/federfall/geocode/. /api/collections/users/auth-with-password has no server-side brake at all, so an anonymous attacker can brute-force or credential-stuff against a self-hosted instance unthrottled. There is no lockout hook either — the only auth-side control is authRule = 'is_active = true'. It is also actively misleading: an operator who opens the admin UI sees rate limiting turned on.

The in-code comment ('start from a clean slate so ONLY the geocode routes get limited... normal API traffic stays unthrottled') shows the wipe is intentional for the geocode budget, but the auth consequence looks unintended.

Fix: when the limiter was previously disabled, keep settings.rateLimits.rules as the baseline instead of replacing it with [] (merge the geocode labels into the existing defaults), or add explicit auth rules alongside them. Worth a test: hammer auth-with-password and assert a 429 arrives.

Many UI screens use bare 'on Object' to catch all exceptions and show a generic message:

```dart
} on Object {
  if (!mounted) return;
  setState(() {
    _busy = false;
    _error = l10n.errorGenericTitle;
  });
}
```

Acceptable in Flutter (no checked exceptions) and good for security (generic user-facing messages via errorGenericTitle), but it masks unexpected runtime errors and makes debugging harder.

Recommendation: in 'on Object' blocks, capture the error without surfacing it to users — e.g. FlutterError.reportError or an analytics/crash-reporting event — so unexpected errors are observable.

Files: multiple UI screens.
Source: code review finding #3 (Code Quality).
